+ * The point is what does NOT happen in the RX callback. Reassembly runs in
+ * atomic context, so it used to finish a message by asking the allocator for a
+ * multi-page block with GFP_ATOMIC -- an order-8 request for 1 MiB, which
+ * cannot reclaim and so fails on a fragmented host with gigabytes still free.
+ * That failure then delivered a short message as a complete one. Handing the
+ * filled buffer to the reader and popping a spare off this stack keeps the
+ * whole path allocation-free and copy-free.
